Cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurements were used to determine the frontier molecular orbitals of the copolymers in the thin films. The measurements were carried out under an inert atmosphere by using tetra-nbutylammoniumhexafluorophosphate $\mathrm{\Phi_{(n-Bu_{4}N P F_{6}}}$ , 0.1M in acetonitrile) as the supporting electrolyte with a glass carbon working electrode, a platinum wire counter electrode, and an $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ electrode as the reference electrode. The ferrocene/ferrocenium $\left(\mathrm{Fc/Fc^{+}}\right)$ reference was used as an internal standard, which was assigned an absolute energy of $-4.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ vs vacuum level.26 Under the same experimental conditions, the onset potential of ${\mathrm{Fc/Fc}}^{+}$ was measured to be 0.47 $\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ with respect to the $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ reference electrode. Based on the onset reduction potential with respect to the standard oxidation potential of the $\mathrm{Fc/Fc^{+}}$ , the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital  energy levels $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{LUMO}})$ and the highest occupied molecular orbital energy levels $(\mathrm{E_{HOMO}})$ of the copolymers were calculated as $\mathrm{E_{LUMO}=-e}$ $\left(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{re}}+4{\cdot}33\right)$ $\ensuremath{(\mathrm{eV})}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaE_{HOMO}=}$ -e $\left(\operatorname{E}_{\operatorname{ox}^{+}4\cdot33}\right)$ (eV) respectively,27 where $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{re}}$ (or $\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{ox}}\mathrm{,}$ ) is the onset of the reduction (or oxidation) potential vs the $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ reference electrode. The respective LUMO values of PNDIT-F8, PNDIT- $\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ , and PNDIT- $\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{-}\mathrm{Br}$ were estimated to be $-3.89$ , -3.91 and -4.18 eV (Table 1). The relative lower LUMO value of PNDIT- $\mathrm{\cdotF3N\mathrm{-}B r}$ may have been caused by electrostatic and anion-induced polarization due to the anion- $\pi$ interaction between the bromine anion and the electron deficient aromatic units.28 The low-lying LUMOs of polymers PNDIT- $\mathrm{.F}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ and PNDIT- $\mathrm{\cdotF3N\mathrm{\cdotBr}}$ were very close to those of PCBM and $\mathrm{PC}_{\mathrm{7}}\mathrm{BM}$ , which can facilitate electron transportation from the fullerene acceptor to the cathode side. From the onset oxidation potential, the HOMO energy levels of PNDIT$\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ and PNDIT-F8 were determined to be $-5.55\mathrm{eV}$ and - 5.63 eV, respectively. It is worth noting that no signal for the oxidation of the pendant amino group was detected for PNDIT- $\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ , which may be explained by the lone pair electrons on the nitrogen atom being prone to transfer onto the electron-deficient NDIs and leading to the formation of charge transfer complexes, a similar phenomenon was also observed in previous reports.5,29 Polymer PNDIT- $\cdot\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Br}$ exhibited a clear oxidation process of the bromine anion with an onset oxidation potential of around 0.5 V before the oxidation of a conjugated main chain, which was verified by the oxidation of tetrabutylammonium bromide at the same applied potential in solution (see Figure S6), indicating that the bromine anions had much better electrondonating ability than the nitrogen atoms. Therefore, the HOMO level of the conjugated main chain of PNDIT$\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{N-Br}$ was calculated from the second onset oxidation potential and estimated to be -5.47 eV. These low-lying HOMO levels of the conjugated main chains indicate that the two WSCPs possess hole-blocking properties with respect to various donor materials in the photovoltaic devices.30
We further investigated the external quantum efficiency (EQE) of devices with various ETL thicknesses. In Figure 2b, a major decline in the EQE can be observed in the 500-800 nm section, which is exactly covered by the ICT absorption band of PNDIT- $\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ . Thereby, we supposed that the suppressed reflectance in the 500–800 nm range for devices with thicker ETLs might be responsible for the reduced $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{sc}}$ . Nevertheless, the thick BHJ composite layers play a more significant role in $R_{2}{\mathrm R}$ manufacturing processing, and thus the potential of these ETLs was further evaluated for broader device application. We then more intensively investigated another BHJ system $\mathrm{(PffBT4T\mathrm{\overline{{\Omega}}O D/P C_{7}B M)}}$ , which could be processed in a wide thickness to minimize the absorption loss in the active layer. As shown in Table 3, there was less than $20\%$ loss of PCE for devices fabricated with ETL thicknesses ranging from $5\:\mathrm{nm}$ to 100 nm for PNDIT- $\mathrm{\cdotF3N\mathrm{\cdotBr}}$ , and for PNDIT- $\mathrm{F}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ , the corresponding loss of PCE was around $15\%$ . In the $\mathrm{PTB}_{7^{-}}\mathrm{Th}/\mathrm{PC}_{7^{1}}\mathrm{BM}$ system, the loss is around $50\%$ , and moreover, the EQE spectrum data of the $\mathrm{PffBT}_{4}\mathrm{T}\mathrm{-OD/PC_{7}B M}.$ based devices further demonstrate that with the thicker film of the active layer the effect of the thickness of the ETL on $\mathrm{J}_{\mathrm{sc}}$ will be reduced due to the light being absorbed in the active layer (see Figure 2e). These results indicate that the newly synthesized $\mathbf{n}$ -type WSCPs are a promising type of ETL for the $R_{2}{\mathrm R}$ processing of PSCs. Consequently, a prominent PCE of $8.04\%$ $(\mathrm{J_{sc}}=14.58~\mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}$ , $\mathrm{V_{oc}}=\mathbf{o.}_{77}\mathrm{V}$ , $\mathrm{FF}=72.72\%,$ ) with an ETL thickness of 100nm was achieved and an outstanding PCE of $10.11\%$ $\mathrm{(J_{sc}=17.91~m A/c m^{2}}$ , $\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{oc}}=\mathbf{o}.77$ V, $\mathrm{FF}~=~73{\cdot}49^{0}6)$ with optimized ETL thickness was realized. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report of WSCP ETLs that can be processed in 100nm thickness and in which the PCE still surpasses $8\%$ .
